---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import BubbleLevelFaq from '../../../components/pages/onlinebubblelevel/BubbleLevelFaq.astro';
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from '../../../i18n/utils';

const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);

const keywords =
  'online bubble level, bubble level, free bubble level, bubble level ruler, bubble level online, spirit level online, vertical level tool online, level checker tool online, leveler tool, bubble level check online, water level tool online, online level bubble level, free online level, online bubble level free, bubble level check';

const title = 'How to Level Furniture, Shelves and Appliances with a Phone';
const description =
  'Level furniture, shelves and appliances with a phone in minutes. Step-by-step checks with an online bubble level, plus fixes for a wobble and a sloped floor.';

const breadcrumbLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://instanten.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Online Bubble Level', item: 'https://instanten.com/onlinebubblelevel/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://instanten.com/onlinebubblelevel/blog/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 4, name: 'How to Level Furniture, Shelves and Appliances with a Phone', item: 'https://instanten.com/onlinebubblelevel/blog/level-furniture-shelves-appliances/' },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  keywords={keywords}
  type="article"
  ogImage="/og-bubble-level.png"
  ogImageAlt={title}
  breadcrumbLd={breadcrumbLd}>
  <Nav />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-[48rem] px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24">
    <a
      href={translatePath('/onlinebubblelevel/blog/')}
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-body hover:text-ink transition-colors mb-8">← Back to Bubble Level Blog</a
    >
    <h1 class="font-sans text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-2">
      How to Level Furniture, Shelves and Appliances with a Phone
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono mb-8">November 13, 2026</p>

    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">A <strong>bubble level online</strong> check is the fastest way to fix the small annoyances of a home: the table that rocks, the shelf that looks drunk, the washing machine that walks. All three have the same cause — a surface or a foot that is out by less than a degree — and all three take minutes once you can see the number.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">This guide takes you from "something looks wrong" to a finished fix. It covers what to check before you start, three jobs worked through step by step, and the fixes that actually hold. It uses the free <strong>online bubble level</strong> on this site, but any phone level app behaves the same way.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      What you need
    </h2>

    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li><strong>Your phone</strong> with the level page open and motion access allowed</li>
      <li><strong>A small spirit level</strong> if you have one — it makes a good second opinion</li>
      <li><strong>Shims</strong>: folded card, plastic shims, or thin felt pads</li>
      <li><strong>A tape measure</strong>, and a screwdriver if feet need adjusting</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">That is it. No app store trip, no download, and nothing to install — which is why a <strong>free bubble level</strong> in a browser tab is easier to reach for than a level in a drawer. It is also <strong>online bubble level free</strong> of charge, with no account, so nothing stands between the question and the number. Nothing about the setup is hidden: open the page, allow motion access, and you are running the same <strong>online level check</strong> you would have tapped anyway.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 1: Check the surface, then the object
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Before you blame the furniture, find out whether the floor or the counter is the problem. A <strong>level checker tool online</strong> reading on the floor tells you which side of the room is low; a reading on the object tells you whether it matches. Two numbers, ten seconds, and the whole diagnosis changes. This is the step most <strong>bubble level check</strong> guides rarely mention, and it is the one that saves the most time.
    </p>

    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li>Place the phone on the floor next to the item and let the bubble settle.</li>
      <li>Note the reading and which end is low.</li>
      <li>Place the phone on the item itself in the same orientation.</li>
      <li>If both readings agree, the floor is sloping. If they disagree, the item is out of level relative to the floor. Cross-check the second reading with a <strong>spirit level online</strong> view on another device if you have one within reach.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">This is the step people skip, and it is the reason some tables get "fixed" twice. A table on a sloping floor can be perfectly square and still rock.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 2: Level a shelf or a floating picture
    </h2>

    <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Shelves</h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Mark the height at both ends before you drill, then set the bracket at the low end first if the wall itself is out. A <strong>vertical level tool online</strong> check on the wall shows you whether the wall leans — older houses often do, and no bracket design compensates for it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Drill level, then install, then re-check with the phone on the shelf. If the shelf reads 0.5° or less, live with it. Anything above 1° shows as objects sliding, and it is worth re-drilling before you load it. On anything longer than about 40 cm a <strong>bubble level ruler</strong> will see a slope the phone rounds away, so borrow one for the final check.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Pictures and mirrors</h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Hang, check the top edge with the phone held flat against it, then step back. The eye is very good at spotting a picture that is 1.5° off, and a phone catches it before you make the hole. For a heavy mirror, check the bracket rather than the frame — frames are often out of square by more than the wall is.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 3: Level furniture
    </h2>

    <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">A table that rocks</h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Turn the table upside down if you can and check the frame. Adjust the feet until the bubble sits centred, then flip it back and test with a glass of water. A wobble that survives this is usually a twisted frame rather than a bad foot, and twisting a frame back is a job for a clamp and patience rather than shims. Run one <strong>bubble level check online</strong> on the frame before you touch the feet, so you know which problem you have.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">A bookcase against a wall</h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Check the vertical axis on the front edge, then the horizontal on a shelf. Most bookcases lean because the floor is out, so pack the low side with a shim rather than shaving the high side. Fix the bookcase to the wall once it stands straight — a level bookcase is still a tipping hazard.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Chairs and desks</h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">A <strong>leveler tool</strong> on the seat tells you which corner is low in seconds. Felt pads stacked two deep are usually enough. If a desk rocks by more than 2°, check whether the legs are screwed in evenly before you shim anything.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 4: Level appliances
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Washing machines are the one appliance where a small error really matters. A machine out by 1° walks across the floor on a spin cycle, and the constant vibration shortens the life of the drum bearing.
    </p>

    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li>Switch the machine off and unplug it.</li>
      <li>Place the phone on the top plate, flat, away from the control panel.</li>
      <li>Turn the front feet until both axes read 0.0° – 0.3°.</li>
      <li>Tighten the lock-nuts against the body so the feet cannot creep.</li>
      <li>Run a spin cycle and re-check — a machine that shakes itself out of level needs the drum load rebalanced.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Fridges and freezers follow the same routine, with one addition: most manufacturers want a slight backward tilt so the doors close on their own. Check the manual, and if it asks for that tilt, use the degrees rather than guessing. For long runs of cabinets, the old <strong>water level tool online</strong> trick is still the most reliable transfer method, with the phone used to confirm each end.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">For an oven or a dishwasher, level first and align the door second. A dishwasher that is level but whose door falls open was aligned to a crooked floor, and the fix is at the feet, not the hinge.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Worked example: level a shelf on a sloping floor
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">The kitchen floor slopes 1° toward the door. You want a shelf on the wall above the counter, and you want it level in the real world, not relative to the floor.
    </p>

    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li>Check the floor with the <strong>online bubble level</strong> and note 1.0° low at the door end.</li>
      <li>Check the wall with the vertical view — it reads 0.2°, so the wall is close to plumb.</li>
      <li>Mark both bracket heights from the same reference line, not from the floor.</li>
      <li>Drill, install, and check the shelf; it should read under 0.3°.</li>
      <li>Confirm with a second reading rotated 180° to rule out a biased phone, and compare the <strong>online level bubble level</strong> number at both ends of the shelf.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Troubleshooting
    </h2>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
        <thead><tr class="bg-canvas-elevated"><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Problem</th><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Likely cause</th><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Fix</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Table still rocks after shimming</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Twisted frame, not a short foot</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Check diagonals with a tape; clamp and re-glue rather than shim</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Shelf reads level but objects slide</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Shelf is level, floor is not, objects follow the floor</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Re-check the floor reading and expect a compromise</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Washing machine walks after levelling</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Lock-nuts not tightened, or an unbalanced load</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Tighten the feet, redistribute the wash, run a spin cycle</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Phone says 0.0° everywhere</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Motion permission blocked or sensor asleep</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Reload the page and allow motion access</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Two readings, same spot, different numbers</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Phone not settled or resting on a camera bump</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Wait five seconds, use the flat part of the case</td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      The short version
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Check the surface before you blame the furniture, fix the low end rather than the high end, and confirm with a second reading rotated 180°. A <strong>free online level</strong> you have calibrated handles shelves, tables, chairs and washing machines; on long spans let a real level make the final call. A calibrated phone gets you within half a degree, which is enough for shelves, tables and washing machines. Open the <strong>online bubble level</strong>, take two readings, and the job takes ten minutes instead of an afternoon — a <strong>bubble level check</strong> is cheaper than re-hanging the same shelf twice.
    </p>

  <BubbleLevelFaq heading="Frequently asked questions about the online bubble level" />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
